   "primary": true,
   "shortTitle": "Convergence, one-sided Lipschitz",
   "shortNumber": "(5.2)",
   "figures": [],
   "sourceLine": 1164,
   "statementHtml": "<p>Three of our arguments use nothing beyond <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:osl\" data-label=\"eq:osl\">(5.1)</a>. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#t.w1\" data-label=\"thm:W1-convergence\">Theorem&nbsp;2.1</a> relies on the inequality <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\frac{\\diff}{\\diff t}|x-y|\\le L(t)|x-y|+|r|\"></span> between a discrete trajectory <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\dot x=v(t,x)+r\"></span> and an exact one, which is <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:osl\" data-label=\"eq:osl\">(5.1)</a> followed by Gronwall. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#t.feedback\" data-label=\"thm:alpha-feedback\">Theorem&nbsp;3.1</a> controls the transport term through <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:T_bound_CS\" data-label=\"eq:T_bound_CS\">(3.5)</a>, in which only <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\|v(t,\\cdot)\\|_{L^\\infty}\"></span> appears. The inequality <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:adaptive-global-energy-inequality\" data-label=\"eq:adaptive-global-energy-inequality\">(4.13)</a> survives once the integration by parts of <a class=\"ref ref-section\" href=\"#s.4\" data-label=\"subsec:adaptive-energy\"><strong>Section&nbsp;4</strong></a>, which uses <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\operatorname{div}v\"></span>, is replaced by a summation over the faces of the tessellation, on each of which <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:osl\" data-label=\"eq:osl\">(5.1)</a> may be applied to the two generators sharing it; this gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\mathscr T_v\\le4L(t)\\mathscr F\"></span>.</p>\n<p>What does not survive is the separation of the generators, which in <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#t.adaptive\" data-label=\"thm:adaptive-energy-convergence\">Theorem&nbsp;4.1</a> came from a lower bound on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(v_i-v_j)\\cdot(x_i-x_j)\"></span> that <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:osl\" data-label=\"eq:osl\">(5.1)</a> does not provide. Collisions do in fact occur. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(\\R/\\mathbb Z)^2\"></span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v(x,y)=(2x-\\operatorname{sign}x,0)\"></span> is one-sided Lipschitz with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"L=2\"></span>, and moves two generators at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(\\pm s,0)\"></span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"0&lt;s&lt;1/4\"></span>, according to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\dot s=2s-1+2^{5/4}(1/4-s)^3\\le-\\frac12+2^{-19/4}\"></span>. The remedy is to replace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v\"></span> by the smoothed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v^\\varepsilon=\\eta_\\varepsilon*v\"></span>, which is Lipschitz for each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\varepsilon&gt;0\"></span> and satisfies <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:osl\" data-label=\"eq:osl\">(5.1)</a> with the same <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"L\"></span>. We use <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\alpha_h=\\mathscr G/h^{5/2}\"></span>, which is locally Lipschitz by <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#r.lipschitz\" data-label=\"rem:lipschitz\">Remark&nbsp;A.2</a>. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#t.adaptive\" data-label=\"thm:adaptive-energy-convergence\">Theorem&nbsp;4.1</a> then gives the convergence estimate for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v^\\varepsilon\"></span>, with constants independent of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\varepsilon\"></span>, thanks to the one-sided energy bound above. Averaging <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:osl\" data-label=\"eq:osl\">(5.1)</a> against <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\eta_\\varepsilon\"></span> bounds the distance between the flows of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v^\\varepsilon\"></span> and of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v\"></span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"O(\\varepsilon^{1/2})\"></span>. With initial data as in <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:adaptive-initial-energy\" data-label=\"eq:adaptive-initial-energy\">(4.1)</a> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\mu(t):=\\Phi(t,\\cdot)_\\#(\\rho_0\\,\\diff x)\"></span>, we obtain</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\" data-tex=\"\\sup_{t\\in[0,T]}W_1\\left(\\mu^{h,\\varepsilon}(t),\\mu(t)\\right)\\le C\\|\\rho_0\\|_{L^1}\\left(h^{1/4}+\\varepsilon^{1/2}\\right) \\tag{5.2}\" id=\"eq:osl-convergence\" data-labels=\"eq:osl-convergence\"></div>\n<p>for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"h,\\varepsilon\\in(0,1]\"></span>, so that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\varepsilon(h)=h\"></span> retains the rate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"O(h^{1/4})\"></span>.</p>",
   "summaryHtml": "<p>For a one-sided Lipschitz velocity on a flat torus, run the dynamics of <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#t.adaptive\" data-label=\"thm:adaptive-energy-convergence\">Theorem&nbsp;4.1</a> with the mollified velocity <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v^\\varepsilon=\\eta_\\varepsilon*v\"></span> and the feedback <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\alpha_h=\\mathscr G/h^{5/2}\"></span>. Then the histogram satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\sup_tW_1(\\mu^{h,\\varepsilon}(t),\\mu(t))\\le C\\|\\rho_0\\|_{L^1}(h^{1/4}+\\varepsilon^{1/2})\"></span> <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:osl-convergence\" data-label=\"eq:osl-convergence\">(5.2)</a>, with constants independent of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\varepsilon\"></span>; the choice <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\varepsilon=h\"></span> gives back the rate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"h^{1/4}\"></span>.</p>",
   "ideaHtml": "<p>Three arguments survive as they stand: the Gronwall comparison of trajectories, the Cauchy–Schwarz bound on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\mathscr T_v\"></span>, and the energy inequality, once integration by parts is replaced by the face summation of <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#s.B\" data-label=\"sec:appendix-osl\">Appendix&nbsp;B</a>. Separation does not survive: the collision example shows two generators meeting in finite time. Mollification repairs it: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v^\\varepsilon\"></span> is Lipschitz, so <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#t.adaptive\" data-label=\"thm:adaptive-energy-convergence\">Theorem&nbsp;4.1</a> applies, and it satisfies <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:osl\" data-label=\"eq:osl\">(5.1)</a> with the same <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"L\"></span>, so the energy and transport bounds hold with constants independent of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\varepsilon\"></span>. Finally the flows of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v^\\varepsilon\"></span> are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"O(\\varepsilon^{1/2})\"></span> apart, and the triangle inequality gives <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:osl-convergence\" data-label=\"eq:osl-convergence\">(5.2)</a>.</p>",
   "where": "§5",
   "children": [],
   "usedBy": [],
   "uses": [
    "d.osl",
    "t.w1",
    "t.feedback",
    "t.adaptive",
    "e.face-osl",
    "e.mollify",
    "x.collision",
    "r.lipschitz"
   ]
