   "shortTitle": "Euler equations, shock-tube test",
   "figures": [],
   "sourceLine": 1196,
   "statementHtml": "<p>We illustrate the effect of the Lloyd correction on a standard test case for the compressible Euler equations</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\" data-tex=\"\\begin{align}\n\\partial_t \\rho + \\nabla \\cdot(\\rho v) &amp;= 0, \\tag{6.1}\\\\\n\\partial_t(\\rho v) + \\nabla \\cdot(\\rho v\\otimes v + p I_2) &amp;= 0, \\tag{6.2}\\\\\n\\partial_t\\left(\\rho E\\right) + \\nabla \\cdot\\left(\\left(\\rho E + p\\right)v\\right) &amp;= 0, \\tag{6.3}\\end{align}\" id=\"eq:mass\" data-labels=\"eq:mass eq:mom eq:energy\"></div>\n<p>where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\rho\"></span> is the density, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v=(v_1,v_2)\"></span> the velocity, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"p\"></span> the pressure, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"I_2\"></span> the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2\\times2\"></span> identity matrix, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"E=e+\\frac12|v|^2\"></span> the total specific energy, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"e\"></span> being the internal energy. The first equation is the continuity equation <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:continuity\" data-label=\"eq:continuity\">(2.1)</a>. The system is closed by an equation of state; for an ideal gas, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"p=(\\gamma-1)\\rho e\"></span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\gamma&gt;1\"></span>. We impose the impermeability condition <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v\\cdot n=0\"></span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(0,T)\\times\\partial\\Omega\"></span>, and the domain is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\Omega=(0,1)^2\"></span>. There is a substantial existence theory for <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:mass\" data-label=\"eq:mass\">(6.1)</a>–<a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:energy\" data-label=\"eq:energy\">(6.3)</a>, ranging from local smooth solutions to weak and entropy solutions; see for instance [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#bib/Dafermos2016\" data-key=\"Dafermos2016\" title=\"Constantine M. Dafermos. Hyperbolic Conservation Laws in Continuum Physics, volume 325 of Grundlehren der mathematischen Wissenschaften. Springer, 4 edition, 2016.\">5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#bib/Majda1984\" data-key=\"Majda1984\" title=\"Andrew Majda. Compressible Fluid Flow and Systems of Conservation Laws in Several Space Variables, volume 53 of Applied Mathematical Sciences. Springer, 1984.\">29</a>].</p>\n<p>The scheme of [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#bib/despres2024\" data-key=\"despres2024\" title=\"Bruno Després. Compressible Lagrangian Voronoi meshes and particle dynamics with shocks. Computer Methods in Applied Mechanics and Engineering, 419:116648, 2024.\">8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#bib/desprem\" data-key=\"desprem\" title=\"Bruno Després and Ronald Remmerswaal. Remarks on a new particle method. Journal of Computational Physics, 523:113662, 2025.\">9</a>] is at the same time a particle method, in the sense of <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:rho-def\" data-label=\"eq:rho-def\">(1.1)</a>–<a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:lag-step-0\" data-label=\"eq:lag-step-0\">(1.2)</a>, and a finite volume method on the Voronoï tessellation of the particles, which is recomputed at every time step. The velocities of the particles are computed with a Riemann solver, for which we refer to those references.</p>\n<p>The initial data are those of a Riemann problem, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\rho_L=1\"></span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v_L=(0,0)\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"p_L=1\"></span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x&lt;0.5\"></span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\rho_R=0.125\"></span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"v_R=(0,0)\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"p_R=0.1\"></span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x&gt;0.5\"></span>, independently of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"y\"></span>. The solution is a weak solution consisting of a shock, followed by a contact discontinuity and a rarefaction fan. The velocity jumps across the shock, so this example lies outside the hypotheses of <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#t.w1\" data-label=\"thm:W1-convergence\">Theorem&nbsp;2.1</a>; the figures below illustrate the effect of the Lloyd correction, but the convergence theorem does not apply to them. The strong gradients also trigger numerical instabilities, the particles tending to come extremely close to one another; see [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#bib/despres2024\" data-key=\"despres2024\" title=\"Bruno Després. Compressible Lagrangian Voronoi meshes and particle dynamics with shocks. Computer Methods in Applied Mechanics and Engineering, 419:116648, 2024.\">8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#bib/desprem\" data-key=\"desprem\" title=\"Bruno Després and Ronald Remmerswaal. Remarks on a new particle method. Journal of Computational Physics, 523:113662, 2025.\">9</a>]. In [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#bib/kincl\" data-key=\"kincl\" title=\"Ondrej Kincl, Ilya Peshkov, and Walter Boscheri. Semi-implicit lagrangian voronoi approximation for the incompressible navier–stokes equations. International Journal for Numerical Methods in Fluids, 97(1):88–115, 2025.\">23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#bib/loulou\" data-key=\"loulou\" title=\"Raphaël Loubère, Pierre-Henri Maire, Mikhail Shashkov, Jérôme Breil, and Stéphane Galera. Reale: A reconnection-based arbitrary-lagrangian–eulerian method. Journal of Computational Physics, 229(12):4724–4761, 2010.\">27</a>], this is mitigated by a regularization of ALE type. Here we rely instead on Lloyd’s algorithm <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:lloyd-step\" data-label=\"eq:lloyd-step\">(1.4)</a>, which acts as an additional repulsive force between the particles.</p>",
   "summaryHtml": "<p>The Euler equations <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:mass\" data-label=\"eq:mass\">(6.1)</a>–<a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:energy\" data-label=\"eq:energy\">(6.3)</a> for an ideal gas on the unit square with impermeable walls; the first equation is the continuity equation <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:continuity\" data-label=\"eq:continuity\">(2.1)</a>. The scheme is a particle method and a finite-volume method at once: the tessellation is recomputed at every step and the velocities come from a Riemann solver. The test is a Riemann problem (a shock, a contact discontinuity and a rarefaction), whose strong gradients drive particles very close to one another; here Lloyd’s step <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:lloyd-step\" data-label=\"eq:lloyd-step\">(1.4)</a> acts as a repulsive force in place of the ALE remeshing used elsewhere.</p>",
   "where": "§6",
   "children": [],
