   "shortTitle": "Cells are Lipschitz in the generators",
   "figures": [],
   "number": "Remark A.2",
   "numberOnly": "A.2",
   "statementHtml": "<p>The maps <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"X\\mapsto V_i(X)\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"X\\mapsto V_i(X)c_i(X)=\\int_{\\Omega_i(X)}x\\,\\diff x\"></span> are locally Lipschitz on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\mathcal U\"></span>, whether or not the Voronoï neighbors change. Indeed, each cell <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\Omega_i(X)\"></span> is the intersection of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\Omega\"></span> with finitely many half-planes, bounded by the bisectors of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x_i\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x_j\"></span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"j\\ne i\"></span> (on the torus, of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x_i\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x_j+k\"></span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"k\\in\\mathcal L\"></span>). These lines depend smoothly on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"X\"></span> as long as the generators stay separated, so the symmetric difference of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\Omega_i(X)\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\Omega_i(X&#x27;)\"></span> has area <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"O(|X-X&#x27;|)\"></span>, locally uniformly on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\mathcal U\"></span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"V_i&gt;0\"></span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\mathcal U\"></span>, the centroids <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"c_i\"></span> and the energy <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\mathscr G\"></span> defined in <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:energy-G\" data-label=\"eq:energy-G\">(1.7)</a> are locally Lipschitz on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\mathcal U\"></span> as well.</p>",
   "sourceLine": 1341,
   "summaryHtml": "<p>Cell volumes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"V_i\"></span> and first moments <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"V_ic_i\"></span> are locally Lipschitz functions of the configuration, even across changes of Voronoï neighbours: each cell is an intersection of half-planes bounded by bisectors that move smoothly with the generators, so the symmetric difference of two cells has area <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"O(|X-X&#x27;|)\"></span>. Since volumes are positive away from collisions, the centroids and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\mathscr G\"></span> are locally Lipschitz too. This is what makes the right-hand sides of <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:feedback-ode\" data-label=\"eq:feedback-ode\">(3.3)</a> and <a class=\"ref ref-eq\" href=\"#eq/eq:adaptive-energy-feedback\" data-label=\"eq:adaptive-energy-feedback\">(4.2)</a> locally Lipschitz, hence the solutions unique.</p>",
   "where": "Appendix A",
